                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f0bd944e-cd44-35d4-9413-75dbdd1774b7",
                        "entry_id": "c8c3b42d-471b-3c1b-a823-8311f889bb87",
                        "glosses": [
                            "устар. единица счёта писчей бумаги, равная 24 листам"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5b315128-5a0f-4430-894a-e1a3b067561a",
                                "sense_id": "f0bd944e-cd44-35d4-9413-75dbdd1774b7",
                                "text": "Чрезвычайно приятно писать к особам, с которыми есть сочувствие, их так мало, так мало, что и дести бумаги не изведёшь в год."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d883d865-dbe9-4690-8889-16c977a10f70",
                                "sense_id": "f0bd944e-cd44-35d4-9413-75dbdd1774b7",
                                "text": "Он потребовал у меня бумаги больше дести и чернил."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "84be27dc-72ed-4dc1-b95a-1faf2e8bc5b1",
                                "sense_id": "f0bd944e-cd44-35d4-9413-75dbdd1774b7",
                                "text": "Усаживался за стол, точно в прострации, клал перед собой стопку чистой бумаги и не поднимая головы изводил десть за дестью."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1df3b892-3d9b-45ec-b307-db8729a15893",
                        "source_entry_id": "c8c3b42d-471b-3c1b-a823-8311f889bb87",
                        "source_sense_id": "f0bd944e-cd44-35d4-9413-75dbdd1774b7",
                        "target_lang_word": "quire",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "quire",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2feb38d9-bb0e-3ee8-af8d-22fb53730220",
                                        "entry_id": "cc29fed0-9727-327a-9ac6-c9fa302ba117",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One-twentieth of a ream of paper; a collection of twenty-four or twenty-five sheets of paper of the same size and quality, unfolded or having a single fold."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fcd29296-b2c5-4868-941e-0dfa1d2321ea",
                                                "sense_id": "2feb38d9-bb0e-3ee8-af8d-22fb53730220",
                                                "text": "Under the year 1533 we are told that the ream contained twenty quires."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e6c5295c-665c-41b8-bf8e-be6e4bdf4fbe",
                                                "sense_id": "2feb38d9-bb0e-3ee8-af8d-22fb53730220",
                                                "text": "[…] and we must accept the fact that all those good novels, Villette, Emma, Wuthering Heights, Middlemarch, were written by women without more experience of life than could enter the house of a clergyman; written too in the common sitting-room of that respectable house and by women so poor that they could not afford to buy more than a few quires of paper at a time upon which to write Wuthering Heights or Jane Eyre."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "31373b93-7e3e-4e4f-a695-8cdee9964f5f",
                                                "sense_id": "2feb38d9-bb0e-3ee8-af8d-22fb53730220",
                                                "text": "We saw above that the fourth quire consists of ten folios, two of which (folios 29 and 31) Richer added to a quaternion (folios 23 to 28, 30, 32). Most of the folios Richer added to his manuscript supplement, elaborate, or amend text that he had already composed in the codex. In this quire, however, Richer wrote around the added folios as if it was the quire he added to them, not the converse. Indeed, if we were to remove folios 29 and 31, there would be neither grammatical nor narrative continuity between the original folios of the quire which would face each other, that is, between folios 28^(v[erso]) and 30^(r[ecto]) on the one hand, or folios 30ᵛ and 32ʳ on the other."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "287e9f09-5999-3246-8f7f-0915a87e918b",
                                        "entry_id": "cc29fed0-9727-327a-9ac6-c9fa302ba117",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A set of leaves which are stitched together, originally a set of four pieces of paper (eight leaves, sixteen pages). This is most often a single signature (i.e. group of four), but may be several nested signatures."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "abbfa500-04c7-3291-b8f9-fa61e312a79b",
                                        "entry_id": "cc29fed0-9727-327a-9ac6-c9fa302ba117",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A book, poem, or pamphlet."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "quire",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e3fd1e05-c69a-3dfb-bbb0-e3ce58366865",
                                        "entry_id": "5a4ed0f2-95a5-3e86-8652-1e2c9c321e2f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To prepare quires by stitching together leaves of paper."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2af3acb8-a065-481f-a8a5-27e33e405bff",
                                                "sense_id": "e3fd1e05-c69a-3dfb-bbb0-e3ce58366865",
                                                "text": "Now, in the first folio volume of 1616, the paging, signatures, and quiring are continuous and regular throughout."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d4de6872-677b-4209-a578-7d1f6a63b2a0",
                                                "sense_id": "e3fd1e05-c69a-3dfb-bbb0-e3ce58366865",
                                                "text": "This is a natural point at which to ask why quiring went out of fashion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ce4398f8-36ca-472c-b897-e8ee636e3883",
                                                "sense_id": "e3fd1e05-c69a-3dfb-bbb0-e3ce58366865",
                                                "text": "By means of these smooth pages we can mostly see how the modern binder made up the book, but whether in doing this he followed the original quiring is quite another matter."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "quire",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "84437248-48bf-36da-b7f5-08ed5d0c0ae4",
                                        "entry_id": "08c0a645-e581-31af-83dc-20c5f861d390",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One quarter of a cruciform church, or the architectural area of a church, generally used by the choir; often near the apse."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b647eb13-565b-307c-8f05-c88ab283a3e4",
                                        "entry_id": "08c0a645-e581-31af-83dc-20c5f861d390",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Archaic form of choir (“group of people who sing together”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "archaic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cdb26bac-481c-411d-bd89-d91bb71d35ac",
                                                "sense_id": "b647eb13-565b-307c-8f05-c88ab283a3e4",
                                                "text": "Madam, myself have lim'd a bush for her,\nAnd plac'd a quire of such enticing birds,\nThat she will light to listen to the lays,\nAnd never mount to trouble you again."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5035a553-e0d0-4434-a84f-84935cd46886",
                                                "sense_id": "b647eb13-565b-307c-8f05-c88ab283a3e4",
                                                "text": "1597–1598, Joseph Hall, Virgidemiarum\nYea, and the prophet of the heav'nly lyre, / Great Solomon sings in the English quire […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "quire",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "25554fc6-a0d9-3295-90d8-2477b83a5645",
                                        "entry_id": "2425c7cf-71f7-3fd0-b393-831c7017c07e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative form of choir (“to sing in concert”)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative",
                                            "poetic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d978546e-a663-4f43-9fd8-1f88a02b0398",
                                                "sense_id": "25554fc6-a0d9-3295-90d8-2477b83a5645",
                                                "text": "Sit, Jessica. Look how the floor of heaven / Is thick inlaid with patines of bright gold: / There's not the smallest orb which thou behold'st / But in his motion like an angel sings, / Still quiring to the young-eyed cherubins; / Such harmony is in immortal souls; / But whilst this muddy vesture of decay / Doth grossly close it in, we cannot hear it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9ebc1cc6-fc36-40db-90c1-c57ecc05cd18",
                                                "sense_id": "25554fc6-a0d9-3295-90d8-2477b83a5645",
                                                "text": "I saw the 'potamus take wing / Ascending from the damp savannas, / And quiring angels round him sing / The praise of God, in loud hosannas."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b3983e58-6b96-4d7f-9f62-c3fe1183d77b",
                                                "sense_id": "25554fc6-a0d9-3295-90d8-2477b83a5645",
                                                "text": "He went on down the hill, toward the dark woods within which the liquid silver voices of the birds called unceasing-the rapid and urgent beating of the urgent and quiring heart of the late spring night."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
